<h2>The question</h2>

<p><a href="/backlog/3605-jit-renumbering-never-re-keys-the-conveyor-queue-sidecar-so/">#3605</a> records a live
failure. Entries in the conveyor's session-local cleared-set sidecar (<code>.conveyor/queue.json</code>) are
still keyed by an item's pre-land birth hash after the drain JIT-numbered that item on land. Every consumer
matches sidecar ids against current numeric ids only, so a genuinely ready item is held as
<code>cleared-but-not-ready</code> and never dispatches. The card leaves one question open: <strong>should
the hash→number translation happen when the rename occurs, or when the stale id is read?</strong> This survey
covers how often the drift happens, which code reads the sidecar, what this repo already does for the same
identity problem elsewhere, and how well-known systems handle a renamed key.</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — a stale hash entry is the normal lifecycle, not an edge case</h2>

<p>A new card is filed under a birth hash and numbered only when its filing PR lands
(<code>scripts/lane-drain.mjs</code>'s <code>numberPendingHashes</code>, which commits
<code>drain: JIT-number &lt;hash&gt;→#&lt;NNN&gt; at land</code> at line 768). The <code>file-item</code>
operation's queue sink (<code>scripts/operations/file-item-io.mjs:76-82</code>) clears the card for the
conveyor <em>at filing time</em>, so the id it stores is necessarily the hash. Any card queued at filing
therefore ends up with a stale sidecar entry the moment it lands. The CLI's own docblock
(<code>scripts/conveyor/queue.mjs:20-23</code>) already describes the drift and tells the operator to
<code>remove</code> and re-<code>add</code> by hand. #3567's fix was exactly that manual step.</p>

