<p>The data needed for a fallback is already loaded. <code>src/_data/backlog.js:357</code> spreads every
frontmatter field (<code>...data</code>) into each loader item, so <code>bornAs</code> is present on the items
<code>dispatch-plan.mjs</code> already loads for scope enrichment (lines 546-555). That load currently happens
<em>after</em> the matching at lines 542-545, so a consumer-side fix has to reorder the two steps.</p>

<h2>Finding 3 — the repo already resolves old hashes at read time in three places</h2>

<ul>
<li><code>scripts/conveyor/queue.mjs:65-88</code> <code>kindOf</code>: matches the filename id first, then falls
back to a <code>bornAs:</code> match, so that a card cleared under its birth hash still resolves after it
lands with a number.</li>
<li><code>scripts/backlog.mjs:1214-1224</code> <code>resolveParent</code>: maps a raw hash that has no file on disk
to its landed number via <code>landedNumberFor</code> (<code>scripts/lane-drain.mjs:785</code>).</li>
<li><code>scripts/conveyor/lease-reaper.mjs:161</code> <code>matchLaneRef</code>: accepts both the digit form and
the hash form of an item id, in one grammar that every consumer shares.</li>
</ul>

<p>The repo's one precedent for renaming at write time is the drain's own <code>applyLedger</code> blind
rewrite (<code>scripts/lane-drain.mjs:704</code>). It is confined to <em>tracked</em> files on purpose. The
doctrine in <code>docs/agent/backlog-workflow.md</code> (line 916) names <code>bornAs</code>-on-main "the sole
cross-clone landed proof," which makes it the durable alias a read-time resolver should use.</p>

<h2>Finding 4 — the "no way to find the sidecar" premise is overstated</h2>

<p>The capture argues that a rename inside <code>numberPendingHashes</code> "has no reliable way to know which
checkout(s) hold a live" sidecar. That was true before #3478. Today
<code>scripts/conveyor/resolve-runner-checkout.mjs</code> finds the one live runner's checkout from its
machine-global lock plus <code>lsof</code>, and both <code>scripts/conveyor/queue-work.mjs:67</code> and
<code>file-item-io.mjs</code> already write to the sidecar it returns. Because the drain daemon is a local KeepAlive job on
the same machine (<code>docs/agent/platform-decisions.md#drain-daemon-self-hosting-boundary</code>, #2501), a write-time rename <em>can</em> reach the sidecar that matters in the common case. It still misses four
cases: no runner is live at land time (<code>file-item-io</code> falls back to its own script-location sidecar),
the runner lock is ambiguous, the hash entries that are already stale today, and a hash an operator types after
land. Each of these still needs a read-time resolver, so write-time renaming can supplement read-time resolution
but cannot replace it.</p>

<h2>Finding 5 — external prior art frames the same axis</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Rename at write time.</strong> SQL <code>ON UPDATE CASCADE</code> rewrites every referencing row when
a key changes. This is sound only when the engine can enumerate all referencing rows, which is precisely what an
untracked, per-checkout sidecar prevents.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the old name as an alias and resolve on read.</strong> HTTP 301/308 permanent redirects (RFC 9110)
keep the old URI working. The spec suggests that clients able to edit links re-link to the new target, but it
does not require the server to find every stale bookmark. GitHub repository renames work the same way: old URLs
and git remotes keep resolving, and updating the remote is recommended, not required. <code>git log
--follow</code> detects renames at read time rather than storing them.</li>
<li><strong>Resolve on read, then write back ("read repair").</strong> Dynamo-style stores such as Cassandra repair
stale replicas during reads. This is safe there because each write carries a timestamp or version, so a repair
cannot overwrite a newer write. <code>scripts/conveyor/queue-store.mjs:154-158</code> says outright that its writer
is unversioned and last-write-wins, "acceptable for a session-local single-operator sidecar". A dispatch tick
that writes back would add a second writer and break that stated assumption.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What this means for the decision</h2>

<p>The prepared item makes two cuts. <strong>Where</strong> the translation happens: at the consumers via
<code>bornAs</code> (recommended), at renumber time, or both. <strong>Who may persist</strong> the canonical id: no
one except an operator's own add or remove (recommended), the dispatch tick through read repair, or an explicit
sweep verb. Whether each consumer gets its own fallback or all of them share one canonicalization step at the
sidecar read boundary is left to the build, and the build should choose the shared step so the readers cannot
disagree. The item carries the code sketch.</p>
